{"reference":"Acts 22","book":"Acts","chapter":22,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Paul's Account and Citizenship","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Acts recounts Paul’s encounter three times, with rhetorical differences among the accounts. In this chapter, his story can be described as conversion, prophetic call, and commissioning, but these overlapping descriptions remain debated. The same narrative repeatedly portrays him as a Roman citizen, although his personal citizenship cannot be independently confirmed.","verse_refs":["Acts 22:3-21","Acts 22:22-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_022_fe2a3c73423b7e52960730bb"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Dependence Within the Call","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul’s inability to see, his being led by others, and Ananias’s restoration of his sight are part of the call narrative. This account treats dependence and care as integral to that narrative; it does not require physical recovery before vocation or identify this event with Paul’s later “thorn.”","verse_refs":["Acts 22:6-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_022_1ba5a1496ca6a7bebe990b05"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"The Legal Turning Point","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s citizenship claim halts the threatened scourging and opens a sequence of hearings, transfers, custody, and eventually an appeal to Caesar. The wider narrative therefore supplies examples of strategic legal claims amid restricted movement, without making every procedure an exact precedent or portraying imperial justice as consistently benevolent.","verse_refs":["Acts 22:22-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_022_fb4d36dd25d43d6c067031fa"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts Historically","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Historical evaluation of Acts is best done claim by claim, comparing particular details with independent evidence rather than assigning the whole work blanket reliability or unreliability.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_022_af7295274c13e4c9b6084e7d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}